kosha beam

  -- a horizontal structural element that carries load by bending.
  -- the beam is the fundamental element of floor and roof construction.
  -- load from above → beam bends → internal stresses develop:
  --   top surface: compression (being squeezed)
  --   bottom surface: tension (being stretched)
  --   neutral axis: middle — no stress
  --
  -- bending moment M: the moment of force that causes bending. M = F × d.
  -- bending stress: σ = My/I where y = distance from neutral axis, I = second moment of area.
  -- deflection: how much the beam sags under load.
  --
  -- I-beam (H-beam): material concentrated at top and bottom flanges.
  -- maximises I (second moment of area) for minimum material.
  -- the web (vertical part) resists shear. the flanges resist bending.
  -- this is why steel structures use I-beams: efficient use of material.
  --
  -- materials: steel beams (strong in tension and compression), reinforced concrete
  -- beams (steel rebar at the bottom where tension occurs), timber beams.

